WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:00.763
Welcome.

2
00:00:00.763 --> 00:00:03.862
This briefing comes from
Book 00 of the Federal

3
00:00:03.862 --> 00:00:07.200
Modernization Workbook,
Orientation and Program.

4
00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:09.213
It's the front door to the series.

5
00:00:09.213 --> 00:00:12.791
It makes the case for modernizing
the client/server estate now,

6
00:00:12.791 --> 00:00:16.025
and it sets out the program
that the other books carry out.

7
00:00:16.025 --> 00:00:17.970
One thing to know before we start:

8
00:00:17.970 --> 00:00:22.075
the workbook is independent technical
guidance, not federal policy.

9
00:00:22.075 --> 00:00:26.413
When it says a law or a memo requires
something, it cites the source.

10
00:00:26.413 --> 00:00:31.200
Everything else, the phases, the gates,
the principles, who decides what,

11
00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:33.511
is the workbook's recommendation:

12
00:00:33.511 --> 00:00:37.638
one way to meet those requirements,
not a requirement itself.

13
00:00:37.638 --> 00:00:41.798
We'll take four questions, why now,
what the program is, who decides,

14
00:00:41.798 --> 00:00:45.850
and how it runs, and finish with
the decisions to make next.

15
00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:50.638
Start with why.

16
00:00:50.638 --> 00:00:54.637
Most federal estates still run
the client/server model they

17
00:00:54.637 --> 00:00:58.637
built in the early 2000s, with
Active Directory at the center.

18
00:00:58.637 --> 00:01:01.963
And the directory has been doing
more than anyone wrote down.

19
00:01:01.963 --> 00:01:07.475
Putting a user or a computer in an organizational
unit, an organizational unit (OU),

20
00:01:07.475 --> 00:01:11.508
quietly decided several things
at once: who administers it,

21
00:01:11.508 --> 00:01:15.869
which Group Policy applies, what it
can reach, how it's named in DNS,

22
00:01:15.869 --> 00:01:19.325
and whether it's trusted
through its certificates.

23
00:01:19.325 --> 00:01:20.537
None of that was wrong.

24
00:01:20.537 --> 00:01:24.926
It just was never recorded anywhere
else, and the cloud doesn't inherit it.

25
00:01:24.926 --> 00:01:28.258
Entra ID's administrative
units can't be nested,

26
00:01:28.258 --> 00:01:31.250
and policy follows groups, not containers.

27
00:01:31.250 --> 00:01:35.168
So an agency that moves the users
and retires the forest without

28
00:01:35.168 --> 00:01:39.013
recovering those decisions isn't
modernizing its governance.

29
00:01:39.013 --> 00:01:40.137
It's deleting it.

30
00:01:40.137 --> 00:01:42.580
The second reason is the calendar.

31
00:01:46.330 --> 00:01:49.268
And the calendar isn't
the agency's any more.

32
00:01:49.268 --> 00:01:52.117
Policy and vendors set
the dates from outside.

33
00:01:52.117 --> 00:01:57.029
Some have already passed:
M-22-09's Zero Trust actions were

34
00:01:57.029 --> 00:02:00.755
due by the end of fiscal 2024,
and they're still in force.

35
00:02:00.755 --> 00:02:02.005
Others are close.

36
00:02:02.005 --> 00:02:07.667
FedRAMP's Consolidated Rules become
mandatory on January 1, 2027.

37
00:02:07.667 --> 00:02:15.479
BOD 26-02 wants listed end-of-support
edge devices gone by February 5, 2027.

38
00:02:15.479 --> 00:02:20.684
And by the program's own count,
not a date OMB published,

39
00:02:20.684 --> 00:02:26.480
the Agency Logging Plan under
M-26-14 is due November 18, 2026.

40
00:02:26.480 --> 00:02:30.793
Vendors add their own
clock: Windows Server 2016,

41
00:02:30.793 --> 00:02:36.830
which runs many domain controllers,
ends support on January 12, 2027.

42
00:02:36.830 --> 00:02:38.505
The slide lists the rest.

43
00:02:38.505 --> 00:02:40.342
Nothing fails on a single date.

44
00:02:40.342 --> 00:02:44.700
But every one of these deadlines
assumes the agency knows what it runs,

45
00:02:44.700 --> 00:02:46.940
who owns it, and what governs it.

46
00:02:50.690 --> 00:02:53.613
Those clocks often get quoted
for more than they say,

47
00:02:53.613 --> 00:02:56.740
so the program plans against
the documents themselves.

48
00:02:56.740 --> 00:02:59.921
For example, FedRAMP
certifies cloud services,

49
00:02:59.921 --> 00:03:03.265
but each agency still
authorizes its own systems.

50
00:03:03.265 --> 00:03:07.665
No federal document requires a
particular Zero Trust maturity stage.

51
00:03:07.665 --> 00:03:13.465
And BOD 25-01 covers cloud tenants,
not the on-premises forest.

52
00:03:13.465 --> 00:03:16.465
So why one program instead
of a set of projects?

53
00:03:16.465 --> 00:03:19.978
Because every project touches
the same hidden decisions.

54
00:03:19.978 --> 00:03:23.214
Intune needs the policy
that Group Policy carried.

55
00:03:23.214 --> 00:03:27.603
Retiring directory DNS needs
the names the forest registered.

56
00:03:27.603 --> 00:03:30.773
Left to each project, those
get answered differently,

57
00:03:30.773 --> 00:03:33.727
or fall between projects
until something breaks.

58
00:03:33.727 --> 00:03:36.617
A program can sequence the
work against the dates;

59
00:03:36.617 --> 00:03:38.802
separate projects can only race them.

60
00:03:38.802 --> 00:03:43.378
It starts with identity, because everything
else decides based on who's asking.

61
00:03:43.378 --> 00:03:45.241
So: fund it as one sequence,

62
00:03:45.241 --> 00:03:50.840
gate on evidence rather than the
calendar, and be honest about maturity.

63
00:03:54.590 --> 00:03:57.202
A quick word on how the
workbook itself works.

64
00:03:57.202 --> 00:04:02.303
It's eighteen books, numbered along the
same dependency chain the program follows:

65
00:04:02.303 --> 00:04:05.378
identity first, then the
estate's infrastructure,

66
00:04:05.378 --> 00:04:08.153
then the boundary,
assurance and operations.

67
00:04:08.153 --> 00:04:12.568
Every book asks the same four
questions, why, what, who and how,

68
00:04:12.568 --> 00:04:16.803
so once you know one, you can
find your way around any other.

69
00:04:16.803 --> 00:04:22.902
And each topic has one home; Book 01,
for example, owns the phases and gates.

70
00:04:22.902 --> 00:04:24.941
Statements come in three kinds.

71
00:04:24.941 --> 00:04:28.159
External facts are footnoted
to a primary source,

72
00:04:28.159 --> 00:04:30.515
checked on the day the book was built.

73
00:04:30.515 --> 00:04:34.677
Program positions are the program's
own view, open to argument.

74
00:04:34.677 --> 00:04:37.680
And illustrative examples
are labeled as such;

75
00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:40.990
the case studies are patterns,
not real engagements.

76
00:04:40.990 --> 00:04:46.140
Each chapter also keeps a verification
file, so every correction is visible.

77
00:04:46.140 --> 00:04:47.900
Now, what is the program?

78
00:04:51.650 --> 00:04:53.525
The program starts with a charter.

79
00:04:53.525 --> 00:04:56.693
It's short on purpose: it's
the page the sponsor signs,

80
00:04:56.693 --> 00:05:00.163
and the page every later
decision gets checked against.

81
00:05:00.163 --> 00:05:05.592
Its purpose is to move the organization
from fragmented, legacy-dependent identity,

82
00:05:05.592 --> 00:05:07.919
policy and addressing to a governed,

83
00:05:07.919 --> 00:05:11.875
attribute-driven hybrid-cloud
model, without rip-and-replace.

84
00:05:11.875 --> 00:05:14.862
It has six objectives,
and the slide lists them.

85
00:05:14.862 --> 00:05:19.860
At their heart: one authoritative source
for structure and identity attributes,

86
00:05:19.860 --> 00:05:25.006
governance rebuilt explicitly instead of
implied, drift treated as a real finding,

87
00:05:25.006 --> 00:05:28.512
and change in controlled waves
that close with evidence.

88
00:05:28.512 --> 00:05:32.341
The scope is the method:
discovery, the target model,

89
00:05:32.341 --> 00:05:36.351
wave plans, change control,
drift detection and gates.

90
00:05:36.351 --> 00:05:41.387
Inline enforcement and the agency's
own cutover are out, at least at first.

91
00:05:41.387 --> 00:05:46.166
And one platform fact shapes the whole
plan: for synced users, the extension

92
00:05:46.166 --> 00:05:51.080
attributes are mastered in Active
Directory and read-only in the cloud.

93
00:05:54.830 --> 00:05:57.846
A sponsor can't sign off on
an outcome that has no test,

94
00:05:57.846 --> 00:06:01.633
so each of the charter's six success
criteria comes with a measure,

95
00:06:01.633 --> 00:06:04.930
the evidence that shows it, and
the gate where it's checked.

96
00:06:04.930 --> 00:06:06.105
Two examples.

97
00:06:06.105 --> 00:06:11.256
The current-state assessment is done
when it covers every forest in scope and

98
00:06:11.256 --> 00:06:16.568
all eleven types of dependency, with no
open priority-one findings, checked at G0.

99
00:06:16.568 --> 00:06:20.342
And the target model is agreed
when every attribute that

100
00:06:20.342 --> 00:06:24.518
drives access has exactly one
designated source, checked at G1.

101
00:06:24.518 --> 00:06:29.634
The others cover a working pilot playbook,
change control on every pilot change,

102
00:06:29.634 --> 00:06:31.804
a response for every kind of drift,

103
00:06:31.804 --> 00:06:35.680
and leadership being able to
explain the path in plain terms.

104
00:06:35.680 --> 00:06:37.880
The numbers are illustrative defaults.

105
00:06:37.880 --> 00:06:43.542
The agency sets its own, writes them down,
and never quietly changes them later.

106
00:06:43.542 --> 00:06:48.240
And these criteria stop at the
pilot; Chapter 10 measures the rest.

107
00:06:51.990 --> 00:06:55.611
Behind the charter sit fifteen
principles, in five groups,

108
00:06:55.611 --> 00:06:58.402
each tied to the book
that carries it in depth.

109
00:06:58.402 --> 00:07:02.646
The foundation group applies to
every change: one source of truth,

110
00:07:02.646 --> 00:07:06.103
change control with provenance,
and drift detection.

111
00:07:06.103 --> 00:07:08.177
The change rule is the strict one.

112
00:07:08.177 --> 00:07:13.105
Every move, add, change, deletion or
reset starts at the source of truth,

113
00:07:13.105 --> 00:07:18.031
carries a real authorization, runs
with just-in-time least privilege,

114
00:07:18.031 --> 00:07:22.353
closes with proof, and produces
its evidence automatically.

115
00:07:22.353 --> 00:07:26.078
Put simply, no closure counts
unless it carries its proof.

116
00:07:26.078 --> 00:07:31.159
The other groups say what replaces the
OU tree, how devices and policy move,

117
00:07:31.159 --> 00:07:35.740
how the HR feed and SQL Server fit
in, and how the program itself runs.

118
00:07:35.740 --> 00:07:40.322
Two source rules were also corrected
against Microsoft's documentation,

119
00:07:40.322 --> 00:07:43.777
including token lifetimes;
the details are in the notes.

120
00:07:43.777 --> 00:07:46.260
Next, the phases and gates.

121
00:07:50.010 --> 00:07:54.910
The plan follows Book 01's model:
seven phases and six gates.

122
00:07:54.910 --> 00:07:59.322
It runs from pre-flight, through
foundation, a pilot division,

123
00:07:59.322 --> 00:08:03.998
general rollout and legacy
retirement, to retiring AD DS itself.

124
00:08:03.998 --> 00:08:08.272
Then comes steady state, which has
no gate; it's a standing rhythm.

125
00:08:08.272 --> 00:08:12.050
Each gate has a plain name, like
Pilot Clean or Legacy Quiet,

126
00:08:12.050 --> 00:08:13.860
and the slide shows them all.

127
00:08:13.860 --> 00:08:16.586
Every phase is bound by
the same constraints:

128
00:08:16.586 --> 00:08:20.932
change starts at the authoritative
source and closes with provenance,

129
00:08:20.932 --> 00:08:24.320
privilege is time-bound,
copies stay non-authoritative,

130
00:08:24.320 --> 00:08:27.635
drift stays visible, and
nothing needs rip-and-replace.

131
00:08:27.635 --> 00:08:31.035
Underneath is one rule:
assess before you act.

132
00:08:31.035 --> 00:08:35.786
The master plan's illustrative fifty-two
weeks stop short of retiring AD DS,

133
00:08:35.786 --> 00:08:39.235
and large federal estates
usually take several years.

134
00:08:39.235 --> 00:08:41.718
Five workstreams run in parallel,

135
00:08:41.718 --> 00:08:45.885
and one dependency never
relaxes: PKI waits for identity.

136
00:08:45.885 --> 00:08:50.400
The gates, not the calendar,
decide when the next phase starts.

137
00:08:54.150 --> 00:08:57.700
Inside the program run seven
complementary projects.

138
00:08:57.700 --> 00:09:01.425
Each has its own charter,
sponsor and success criteria,

139
00:09:01.425 --> 00:09:06.805
and each turns a gap the program often
finds into real work: identity lifecycle,

140
00:09:06.805 --> 00:09:09.702
endpoints, network and access, monitoring,

141
00:09:09.702 --> 00:09:13.675
privileged access, the data
platform, and the supply chain.

142
00:09:13.675 --> 00:09:18.638
Identity goes first, because everything
else uses its attributes and owners.

143
00:09:18.638 --> 00:09:23.875
Privileged access starts early too,
for Tier 0 and emergency access.

144
00:09:23.875 --> 00:09:25.450
And there's a catch worth knowing.

145
00:09:25.450 --> 00:09:30.355
Microsoft recommends at least two cloud-only
emergency accounts with permanent

146
00:09:30.355 --> 00:09:33.456
Global Administrator, so
a target of zero permanent

147
00:09:33.456 --> 00:09:36.413
Global Administrators has
to exempt them by name.

148
00:09:36.413 --> 00:09:38.661
Two source claims have also moved,

149
00:09:38.661 --> 00:09:42.837
around software attestation and
FedRAMP; the notes cover both.

150
00:09:42.837 --> 00:09:47.818
And two rules hold for every project:
none creates a second source of truth,

151
00:09:47.818 --> 00:09:50.512
and none clears a program gate on its own.

152
00:09:50.512 --> 00:09:52.290
So who makes the calls?

153
00:09:56.040 --> 00:09:58.002
Every decision gets one owner.

154
00:09:58.002 --> 00:10:02.225
The executive sponsor and the CIO
own scope, funding and sequence,

155
00:10:02.225 --> 00:10:05.978
and they decide when Active
Directory components retire.

156
00:10:05.978 --> 00:10:08.540
The CISO sets the control requirements.

157
00:10:08.540 --> 00:10:13.027
The steward runs the program office
and owns the plan and the repository.

158
00:10:13.027 --> 00:10:18.343
Each gate is signed by whoever matches
what it ends: the steward clears most of

159
00:10:18.343 --> 00:10:23.491
them, and the sponsor and CIO sign the
last one, the one that says AD is retired.

160
00:10:23.491 --> 00:10:27.804
The book corrects the plans on three
decisions, and one matters most:

161
00:10:27.804 --> 00:10:31.502
residual risk belongs to the
authorizing official alone.

162
00:10:31.502 --> 00:10:35.136
Under the Risk Management Framework
no one else can accept it,

163
00:10:35.136 --> 00:10:38.841
and for federal agencies it's an
inherently governmental role.

164
00:10:38.841 --> 00:10:40.115
And plan for people.

165
00:10:40.115 --> 00:10:44.407
The core team is about five and a
half full-time staff, and the budget

166
00:10:44.407 --> 00:10:49.140
has to cover others too, like
application owners, the help desk and HR.

167
00:10:52.890 --> 00:10:53.828
How does it run?

168
00:10:53.828 --> 00:10:55.803
Each stretch has something to prove.

169
00:10:55.803 --> 00:10:58.898
Pre-flight builds the
platform host as a Tier 0

170
00:10:58.898 --> 00:11:02.402
system and runs a read-only
assessment of the estate.

171
00:11:02.402 --> 00:11:05.188
It produces twenty-three deliverables,

172
00:11:05.188 --> 00:11:08.564
and G0 passes only with
no open critical finding.

173
00:11:08.564 --> 00:11:11.883
Foundation turns on the
Conditional Access baseline,

174
00:11:11.883 --> 00:11:15.127
but only after at least seven
days in report-only mode.

175
00:11:15.127 --> 00:11:19.927
The pilot is one division, measured
against eight numeric criteria, such as

176
00:11:19.927 --> 00:11:25.190
authentication success above 99 percent,
and held drift-free for an agreed period.

177
00:11:25.190 --> 00:11:27.140
Rollout then moves in waves.

178
00:11:27.140 --> 00:11:32.377
Retirement takes things away, so nothing is
retired unless its rollback still works.

179
00:11:32.377 --> 00:11:36.728
And retired disks get sanitized,
because they hold password hashes.

180
00:11:36.728 --> 00:11:41.994
In steady state, reassessment runs weekly
and drift detection daily, and every

181
00:11:41.994 --> 00:11:47.178
severity has a clock: by default, a critical
finding is fixed within four hours.

182
00:11:47.178 --> 00:11:49.180
That leaves the decisions.

183
00:11:52.930 --> 00:11:55.255
So, four decisions to make next.

184
00:11:55.255 --> 00:11:58.078
First, charter the program
and name its people,

185
00:11:58.078 --> 00:12:00.677
starting with the sponsor and the steward,

186
00:12:00.677 --> 00:12:05.580
building on the Zero Trust lead and
identity governance the agency already has.

187
00:12:05.580 --> 00:12:08.316
Second, scope the read-only assessment:

188
00:12:08.316 --> 00:12:11.480
which forests, which
tenants, which accounts.

189
00:12:11.480 --> 00:12:16.484
Third, designate the authoritative
sources: usually HR for employees,

190
00:12:16.484 --> 00:12:21.230
and a separate source for contractors,
whom HR usually doesn't hold.

191
00:12:21.230 --> 00:12:24.126
And fourth, set the
thresholds and evidence rules

192
00:12:24.126 --> 00:12:27.305
before the first change, and
never change them quietly.

193
00:12:27.305 --> 00:12:31.669
The recommended first wave is identity
structure and dynamic membership,

194
00:12:31.669 --> 00:12:34.555
because every later wave
scopes itself with them.

195
00:12:34.555 --> 00:12:39.762
And one clock is close: by the
program's count, the Agency Logging Plan

196
00:12:39.762 --> 00:12:45.155
is due November 18, 2026, so confirm
that date with the CISO's office now.

197
00:12:45.155 --> 00:12:47.967
Recover the governance
once, in one program.

198
00:12:47.967 --> 00:12:52.442
Book 00 has the detail,
with every claim footnoted.

199
00:12:52.442 --> 00:12:53.260
Thank you.
